## At a glance

| Dimension | New Relic | Tracify |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Scope** | Enterprise observability (all signals) | Agent observability |
| **LLM-specific** | No | Yes |
| **Evaluation** | No | Integrated |
| **Cost tracking** | None | Comprehensive |
| **Enterprise features** | Yes (SLAs, alerts, etc.) | Standard |
| **Best for** | Large enterprises | LLM teams |

New Relic is enterprise observability; Tracify is agent-specific.

## Core difference

**New Relic** monitors *entire systems*: infrastructure, applications, errors. **Tracify** monitors *agent behavior*: traces, evaluations, costs.

## Scenario 1: enterprise monitoring

You have complex infrastructure and need comprehensive visibility.

**With New Relic:** Enterprise-grade, proven, integrated with everything.

**With Tracify:** Not designed for enterprise infrastructure.

**Result:** New Relic wins.

## Scenario 2: agent-specific observability

You need traces, evaluation, cost tracking for agents.

**With New Relic:** Generic observability. Evaluation and cost tracking require custom work.

**With Tracify:** Purpose-built for agents.

**Result:** Tracify wins.

## Cost and complexity

**New Relic:** Enterprise pricing, high complexity
**Tracify:** Focused pricing, simple setup

New Relic is overkill for agent-only observability.

## When to choose each

**Choose New Relic:** If you need enterprise observability for infrastructure
**Choose Tracify:** If you need agent observability

## FAQ

{% faq-item question="Does New Relic track LLM costs?" answer="No, New Relic is infrastructure-focused." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can I use both?" answer="Yes, likely already do. New Relic for infrastructure, Tracify for agents." /%}

{% faq-item question="Is New Relic cheaper?" answer="At enterprise scale, no. For agent-only, Tracify is cheaper." /%}

{% faq-item question="Should I replace New Relic?" answer="No, keep it for infrastructure. Add Tracify for agents." /%}

{% faq-item question="When should I choose Tracify?" answer="When agent observability is your primary need." /%}
